            the claims on this page are limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We looked for scales that are sold on Amazon US under the brand's own listing, that an
            independent reviewer had used, and that publish their safety guidance on bioimpedance.
            For each, we read the manual or product page for who should not use body-composition
            mode, and we say on the card, and in the section below, exactly what the maker says. We
            did not rate scales on body-fat accuracy, because no consumer review can establish it
            without a reference method, and makers do not publish comparable validation data.
          </p>
          <p>
            Several familiar names fell out. The <strong>Fitbit Aria Air</strong> did not appear on
            Amazon US when we searched on 19 October; Tom's Guide lists it at Best Buy and suggests
            it only for existing Fitbit users. We found no <strong>Eufy</strong> smart scale on
            Amazon US on that date either. <strong>Withings Body Scan 2</strong>, the newer version
            of the Body Scan, is reviewed by Tom's Guide at $599 but we did not find it on Amazon US,
            so the card is for the original Body Scan, which Amazon itself was selling. The
            <strong>Renpho MorphoScan</strong> is an eight-electrode scale with a handle at a lower
            price than the Body Scan, but Tom's Guide found its in-app analysis inaccurate and often
            contradictory, so we left it out. Etekcity, Arboleaf, FITINDEX and many other budget
            brands sell body fat scales on Amazon; we did not find independent hands-on coverage
            for the specific current models in time to card them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every brand on this page was searched in the CPSC recall database by recall title,
            manufacturer and recall description, along with the terms "scale" and "body
            composition." We found no recall for any scale on this page, and those category
            searches turned up no recall of a bathroom or body fat scale. The only recent scale
            recall they found was a January 2025 recall of Greater Goods digital kitchen scales
            under the federal button-battery safety rules; kitchen scales are not covered here. Wyze recalled a security camera in June
            2026 and Anker, Eufy's parent, has recalled power banks and other battery products; none
