          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How a Body Fat Scale Works</h2>
<p>
            Step on a body fat scale barefoot and it sends a small electrical current, which you
            will not feel, through metal electrodes under your feet, up one leg and down the other.
            Garmin's manual describes exactly this. Water and the lean tissue that holds most of it
            conduct the current well; fat conducts it poorly. The scale measures how much the
            current is impeded, then puts that figure, your weight and the height, age and sex you
            entered into an equation to estimate body fat, muscle, water and bone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three design choices change what the scale can estimate:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Four electrodes or eight.</strong> A standard scale has four, all under your
              feet, so the current mostly travels through your legs and lower body and the rest of
              you is inferred. Scales with a handle, like the Withings Body Scan, add electrodes for
              the hands, which lets them estimate arms, legs and trunk separately.</li>
            <li><strong>Single or multiple frequencies.</strong> Different frequencies pass through
              tissue differently. Withings says its Body Smart uses multi-frequency analysis; most
              budget scales do not say. More frequencies give the equation more information, not a
              direct measurement.</li>
            <li><strong>The equation.</strong> Every maker uses its own, built from reference
              studies on groups of people. If your build is unlike the reference group — very
              muscular, very lean, older, younger — the estimate can be further off, which is why
              several scales offer an athlete mode.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of that makes the scale useless. It means the scale is estimating, and the
            estimate is only as consistent as the conditions you give it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="accuracy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How Accurate Is a Smart Scale? Weight vs Body Fat</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Weight:</strong> as accurate as a good digital bathroom scale. Withings rates the
            Body Smart and Body Scan to 0.1 lb increments, and Renpho the Elis 1 to 0.2 lb. Place any
            scale on a hard, flat floor; carpet makes weight readings unreliable, which is why Garmin
            ships the Index S2 with risers for carpet.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Body fat and the rest:</strong> an estimate that moves with your hydration. Because
            water carries the current, anything that changes how much water you are holding, or
            where, changes the reading. A large drink, a meal, a hard workout, a sauna or a hot
            shower, alcohol the night before, and the fluid shifts many women notice across the
            menstrual cycle can all move the figure by more than your body fat has actually
            changed. Garmin's manual recommends waiting two hours after eating, drinking,
            exercising, bathing or visiting a sauna, and using the scale under the same conditions
            at about the same time of day. Wyze's listing says its body-composition data is for
            long-term trends, not single comparisons, and that surface, posture and foot contact
            affect it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to get a figure worth tracking</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Same time, same state.</strong> First thing in the morning, after using the
              bathroom and before eating, drinking or exercising, is the most repeatable moment for
              most people.</li>
            <li><strong>Barefoot, dry feet, centered on the electrodes.</strong> Socks block the
              current; very dry or calloused skin and damp feet both change contact.</li>
            <li><strong>Watch the weekly average, not the day.</strong> A single reading can swing
              with what you drank last night. A seven-day average moving in one direction over a
              month is a real signal.</li>
            <li><strong>Do not compare scales.</strong> Two brands, or a scale and a gym's machine,
              use different equations and will disagree. Pick one and stay with it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The same honesty applies to every number a fitness device gives you. Our guide to
            <a href="/are-treadmill-calories-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether treadmill calorie counts are accurate</a>
            makes the same point about estimates from models, and our
            <a href="/best-fitness-trackers/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">fitness tracker guide</a>
            does it for heart rate and sleep.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-not-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Who Should Not Use a Body Fat Scale</h2>
<p>
            Bioimpedance passes a current through your body, so every maker on this page tells some
            people not to use the body-composition feature. The groups are similar but the wording
            differs, so here is what each maker says, from the manual or product page we could read
            on 19 October 2026.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Maker</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What it says</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Way to switch the current off</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Withings (Body Smart manual)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Its weight-only mode disables all impedance features "so you can use our scales safely if you have a medical implant"</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Weight-only mode; separate pregnancy and baby modes</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wyze (Scale X guide)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">People with an implanted device such as a pacemaker, an electronic life-support system or a portable electronic medical device such as an electrocardiograph, and pregnant women, must use it only while wearing shoes or in the "Only Measure Weight" setting</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">"Only Measure Weight" setting; pregnancy mode, which Wyze says uses no current</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Renpho (Elis 1 page)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Children under 8, and people who are pregnant or have osteoporosis, a pacemaker, artificial joints, a portable ECG or other metal implants, should use it for weighing only and wear shoes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Wear shoes, per Renpho; we did not find a named weight-only setting</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Garmin (Index S2)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The manual refers to a safety leaflet in the box, which we could not read online; Tom's Guide reports Garmin advises against use with a pacemaker or other internal electronic device and says to consult a doctor if pregnant</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No pregnancy or baby mode, per Tom's Guide; read the leaflet</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Withings (Body Scan)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">We could not read its manual online; Tom's Guide lists pregnancy, baby, athlete and eyes-closed modes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the manual before use, especially with the hand electrodes</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>In plain terms:</strong> if you have a pacemaker, an implanted defibrillator or
            any other implanted or body-worn electronic medical device, do not use body-composition
            mode at all unless your device's manufacturer and your clinician say you may; use weight
            only, or a plain scale. Wyze adds that you should check your medical device maker's
            instructions. If you are pregnant, use weight only or a pregnancy mode that the maker
            says turns the current off, and ask your obstetric care provider what they want you to
            track.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Groups for whom the reading is less meaningful.</strong> Wyze's guide also says
            that people under 18 or over 65, or who are pregnant, have osteoporosis or very low bone
            density, have edema, or are on dialysis should treat the body-fat figure only as a
            reference, because it may be inaccurate for them. The same logic applies to anyone whose
            fluid balance is affected by a medical condition or medication: the scale's equation
            assumes typical hydration, and you may not have it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Weight on its own is safe for everyone on any of these scales once the current is off,
            and for many people the weight trend is the only number worth following anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="metrics" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Which Smart Scale Numbers Are Worth Watching</h2>
<p>
            A scale may show a dozen figures. They are not equally useful.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Weight trend.</strong> The most reliable figure on the scale, and the main
              reason to own a smart one: automatic logging turns noisy daily weights into a trend
              line you can trust.</li>
            <li><strong>Body fat percentage trend.</strong> Useful for direction over weeks, when
              measured the same way every morning. Not useful as an absolute figure to compare with
              a friend, a chart or a gym machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Muscle or lean mass.</strong> Derived from the same reading, so it moves with
              hydration in the same way. A sudden "loss of muscle" after a hard, sweaty session is
              usually water.</li>
            <li><strong>Body water.</strong> The figure the reading is most directly built on, but it
              is still an estimate; do not use it to judge whether you are dehydrated.</li>
            <li><strong>Visceral fat, metabolic age, BMR, bone mass.</strong> Further estimates
              stacked on the first. Treat them as curiosities unless a clinician asks for them.</li>
            <li><strong>Heart rate and ECG features.</strong> The Withings scales read heart rate
              through your feet, and the Body Scan adds an ECG with what Withings describes as
              atrial fibrillation detection. These are the maker's claims; a scale is not a
              substitute for a doctor's assessment, and any symptoms need medical attention whatever
              it shows.</li>
            <li><strong>BMI.</strong> Simple arithmetic from weight and height, so it is as accurate
              as your weight, but it says nothing about body composition. Garmin's manual says its
              BMI categories apply to adults.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are using a scale to follow a weight-loss plan built around walking or running,
            our guides to the
            <a href="/best-treadmill-for-weight-loss/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill for weight loss</a>
            and
            <a href="/does-treadmill-burn-belly-fat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether treadmill work burns belly fat</a>
            are honest about what exercise alone can do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What to Look For in a Smart Scale</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A way to turn the current off</h3>
          <p>
            For a household, this is the first thing to check. If anyone who will use the scale has
            an implanted device or may become pregnant, buy one with a clearly documented weight-only
            or pregnancy mode, such as the Withings or Wyze scales here.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The app and where your data goes</h3>
          <p>
            The scale is a sensor; the app is the product. Check that it syncs with what you already
            use — Apple Health, Health Connect, Fitbit or Google Health, Garmin Connect,
            MyFitnessPal — and look at how it shows trends. Garmin's scale is the obvious choice for
            Garmin watch owners; the Withings app is the one independent reviewers most often praise.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Wi-Fi or Bluetooth</h3>
          <p>
            Wi-Fi scales upload a reading as soon as you step off. Bluetooth-only scales, like the
            Wyze Scale X and Renpho Elis 1, sync when your phone is nearby with the app able to
            connect, which is fine if your phone is in the bathroom and annoying if it is not.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Users and recognition</h3>
          <p>
            Scales recognize users by weight and, on some, by stored profiles. Eight users is common;
            Garmin allows 16 and Renpho unlimited app profiles. Two people of similar weight can be
            confused, so check how the app lets you reassign a reading.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Subscriptions</h3>
          <p>
            Withings sells Withings+, an optional subscription that adds extra scores and coaching;
            Tom's Guide reports it at $99 a year. Basic readings and trends are free on every scale
            here, so treat any subscription as optional.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Capacity, batteries and floor</h3>
          <p>
            Most of these scales are rated to about 400 lb; if you are near the limit, check the exact
            listing, because published figures differ between sources for some models. AAA scales
            run for many months; the Body Scan is rechargeable. Put the scale on hard flooring, and
            keep it in one place so the readings stay comparable. If you are also checking a
            treadmill's limit, our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill weight capacity guide</a>
            explains how those ratings work.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">FSA and HSA</h3>
          <p>
            Several of these listings describe the scale as FSA or HSA eligible. Eligibility depends
            on your plan; our guide to
            <a href="/are-treadmills-hsa-fsa-eligible/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether fitness equipment is HSA/FSA eligible</a>
            explains how plan administrators usually decide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="with-training" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Using a Smart Scale Alongside Your Training</h2>
<p>
            A scale is most useful as one quiet input among several, checked at the same time each
            morning and reviewed weekly, not daily.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Weigh before training, not after.</strong> A long treadmill session or a hot
              home-gym workout can drop several pounds of water, and the body-fat estimate jumps
              around with it.</li>
            <li><strong>Expect strength training to confuse it.</strong> Muscle soreness and the
              water that comes with it can raise weight and shift body-fat estimates for a few days
              after a hard session. If you have just started lifting with, say,
              <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbells</a>,
              give it weeks before reading anything into the trend.</li>
            <li><strong>Pair it with something you can feel.</strong> How your clothes fit, how your
              usual treadmill pace feels, and how much you can lift are useful checks on whatever the
              scale says.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking counts.</strong> If your plan is built around daily walking, our page
              on whether
              <a href="/do-walking-pads-help-you-lose-weight/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads help you lose weight</a>
              sets realistic expectations.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If only weight matters to you,</strong> a plain digital bathroom scale and a note
            on your phone do the job. You give up automatic logging, nothing else.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have an implanted electronic device and no one else in the house needs
            body composition,</strong> buy a weight-only scale and avoid the question entirely.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you need a body-composition figure you can rely on</strong> — for a clinical
            reason, a weight-class sport or a research goal — ask about a reference method such as
            a DEXA scan through a clinician or a university or sports facility. A bathroom scale
            cannot substitute for that.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are most interested in activity and heart rate,</strong> a wearable gives
            you far more for the money; see our
            <a href="/best-fitness-trackers/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best fitness trackers</a>
            and
            <a href="/best-heart-rate-monitors-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">heart rate monitor</a>
            guides.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best smart scale?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Withings Body Smart. It syncs over Wi-Fi, recognizes up to eight users, has pregnancy, baby and athlete modes, and offers a weight-only mode that Withings says lets people with a medical implant use it safely. On a budget, the Wyze Scale X and Renpho Elis 1 cover the basics for about $25 to $40.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are body fat scales accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For weight, yes. For body fat, they give an estimate that is useful as a trend and unreliable as a single number. The scale infers body fat from how a small current passes through you, and that depends on hydration, so a drink, a meal, a workout or a hot shower can move the reading. Measure at the same time each morning and watch the weekly average.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a smart scale with a pacemaker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in body-composition mode unless your device maker and clinician say you may. The scale makers on this page tell people with a pacemaker or other implanted electronic device to use weight only. Withings offers a weight-only mode, Wyze an "Only Measure Weight" setting, and Renpho says to weigh in shoes. Garmin's guidance is in the leaflet in the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a body fat scale while pregnant?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use weight only, or a pregnancy mode that the maker says turns the current off, and ask your care provider what to track. Wyze and Renpho both tell pregnant users not to use body-composition mode, and Wyze says its pregnancy mode uses no current. Withings offers pregnancy and weight-only modes. Tom's Guide reports Garmin advises consulting a doctor first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my smart scale body fat reading change so much?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly because of water. The current a body fat scale uses travels through water in your body, so anything that changes your hydration, such as drinking, eating, exercise, a sauna, alcohol or the menstrual cycle, changes the estimate. Garmin recommends waiting two hours after eating, drinking, exercising or bathing. Weigh at the same time and in the same state each day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do smart scales need a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Every scale on this page shows weight, body-composition estimates and trends in its free app. Withings sells an optional Withings+ subscription for extra scores and coaching, which Tom's Guide reports at $99 a year. You can use any of these scales fully without paying monthly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Smart Scales</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns a useful trend into a misleading number.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using body-fat mode with an implanted device</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">If you have a pacemaker, defibrillator or other implanted or body-worn electronic medical device, the makers on this page say to use weight only. Set up a weight-only profile, or weigh in shoes where that is the maker's instruction, and ask your device manufacturer and clinician before using any body-composition feature.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Weighing right after a workout or a big drink</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Exercise, sweating, a large drink or a meal all change your hydration, and the body-fat estimate moves with it. Weigh first thing in the morning, before eating, drinking or training, barefoot on a hard floor, so each reading is taken in the same state as the last.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing two scales, or a scale with a gym machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Each maker uses its own equation, so two scales can give body-fat readings several points apart for the same person on the same morning. Neither is wrong by its own method. Pick one scale, keep it in one place, and compare it only with itself.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reacting to one day’s number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Daily readings swing with water, food and sleep. A single jump in weight or body fat after a salty dinner or a hard session is not a real change. Look at a seven-day average and the direction over a month before changing anything in your training or eating.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

