        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">For most homes, buy the Withings Body Smart</strong>: accurate
            weight, Wi-Fi sync, eight users and a documented weight-only mode for anyone who should
            not use bioimpedance. On a budget, the Wyze Scale X gives you the same essentials and a
            clear way to switch the current off for about $36, and the Renpho Elis 1 is the cheapest
            way to get a body-fat trend. Garmin owners should choose the Index S2 for the ecosystem.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you buy, trust the weight trend most, read body fat as a direction rather than a
            number, measure at the same time every morning, and turn body-composition mode off if
            you have an implanted device or are pregnant. If activity matters more than body
            composition,
            <a href="/best-fitness-trackers/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">our fitness tracker guide</a>
            is the better place to spend.
          </p>
